# Corporate trust anchors are environment-specific and not ours to distribute.
certs/*.crt
certs/*.pem
!certs/README.md

# Python bytecode. Running the test suite locally produces these throughout the
# tree; without this line they get committed and every subsequent diff is noise.
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*.egg-info/
.pytest_cache/

# Local environment. .env holds API keys and must never be committed;
# .env.example is the template that should be.
.env
!.env.example
case-backups/

# pg_dump backups taken by `make.ps1 bundle-in` before a merge. Working
# database contents - case data, estimates, agent runs - not source. The
# schema under db/init/ stays tracked.
/db-backup-*.sql
_samples_backup/

# Scratch copies of the sample set pulled out of the container during a run.
_s[0-9]*/
_samples_new*/
_delta/

# Real operator performance-management exports, and anything shaped like one.
#
# A carrier PM extract carries device names, resource names and collection
# timestamps for live network elements. It is the operator's data, not ours
# to hold and not ours to distribute, and `make bundle-out` carries every
# byte of this history to whoever receives it.
#
# One such file - 6.5 MB of core trunk counters - sat in a working tree
# beside this repository and was kept out by hand. Nothing stopped it: it
# was untracked but not ignored, so a single `git add -A` would have
# committed it, and removing it afterwards would not remove it from the
# history a bundle ships.
#
# Matched by shape rather than by that one filename, because the next
# export will carry a different date. Deliberately NOT `*.csv` - the
# verification register and the sample summary are tracked CSVs and
# hiding them from `git add` would be its own quiet failure.
pm_core*
*_trunks_*.csv
/operator-data/
